Tables support `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `DROP`, `REFERENCES`, and `TRUNCATE`; functions and
procedures support `EXECUTE`. Grantees can be a user, `ROLE`, `GROUP`, or `PUBLIC`.

## Owner

`owner` selects whose future objects receive the privileges (`FOR USER`). Only a superuser can define defaults for
other users. Omit `owner` to define the defaults of the user the provider connects as, which is what Redshift changes
without `FOR USER`; the tuple then follows that connection user, so a provider configuration that connects as another
user manages that user's defaults instead.

## Grant Options

A `USER` grantee can receive the privileges `WITH GRANT OPTION` on every future object. `grant_option_privileges` owns
which of them carry the option: it must be a subset of `privileges` and defaults to none. Removing a privilege from the
set revokes only its option. Groups, roles, and `PUBLIC` cannot hold grant options.

## Implicit PUBLIC EXECUTE on Functions

Redshift grants `EXECUTE` on every new function to `PUBLIC` without being asked; procedures run only for their owner and
superusers by default. The database-wide tuple `grantee_type = "PUBLIC"`, `object_type = "FUNCTIONS"` without
`schema_name` reports that implicit grant as `EXECUTE`, reading `PG_DEFAULT_ACL` because the catalog has no row for it
until the defaults change. Set `privileges = []` to revoke it, as AWS recommends before granting `EXECUTE` to specific
users or groups. Deleting or replacing that tuple leaves the revocation in place rather than widening access; to return
to the Redshift default, set `privileges = ["EXECUTE"]` and apply, then remove the resource with a `removed` block that
sets `destroy = false`. Schema-specific defaults add to the database-wide ones and cannot remove this grant.

{{ tffile "examples/resources/redshift_default_privileges/public_functions.tf" }}

## Lifecycle and Ownership

Reads `svv_default_privileges` in the target database. Changing another user's defaults requires appropriate SQL
administration privileges. Schema-specific defaults add to database-wide defaults; this resource owns only its exact
tuple. Existing tables/views/functions/procedures are unaffected, including on destroy. Scoped permissions are
different: they cover current and future objects regardless of creator.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the tuple's default privileges and grant options from `SVV_DEFAULT_PRIVILEGES`, and `PG_DEFAULT_ACL`
for the implicit `PUBLIC` default; an update reads them again, runs one `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` statement per
privilege that differs, starting from what the catalog holds, and re-reads the catalog to verify the exact sets; every
change to the tuple itself replaces the grant. No statement touches objects that already exist.

| Change                                                                    | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `database_name`, `schema_name`, or `object_type`                          | replaces the grant: they select other defaults, so the old tuple's defaults are revoked and the new tuple's granted                                                                                        |
| `owner` set, changed, or removed                                          | replaces the grant: the owner is part of the tuple, also when the new value names the user the provider connects as                                                                                        |
| `grantee` or `grantee_type`                                               | replaces the grant: the grantee is part of the tuple                                                                                                                                                       |
| privilege added to `privileges`                                           | `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES [FOR USER ...] [IN SCHEMA ...] GRANT ... ON TABLES`, `ON FUNCTIONS`, or `ON PROCEDURES`                                                                                          |
| privilege removed from `privileges`                                       | `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... REVOKE ... FROM`, which also removes its grant option                                                                                                                        |
| `privileges` set to `[]`                                                  | `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... REVOKE` for every privilege the tuple holds; for the database-wide `PUBLIC` `FUNCTIONS` tuple this includes the implicit `EXECUTE`, also while the catalog has no row for it |
| privilege added to `grant_option_privileges`                              | `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... GRANT ... WITH GRANT OPTION`; a privilege added to both sets at once is granted with the option in one statement                                                             |
| privilege removed from `grant_option_privileges`, or the argument removed | `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... REVOKE GRANT OPTION FOR ...`; the privilege stays                                                                                                                            |

An update revokes before it grants: removed privileges first, then removed grant options, then added privileges, then
added grant options. The statements share no transaction: when one fails, the earlier ones stay applied and the next
plan shows what is left. A catalog privilege outside the object type's list fails the refresh. Planning rejects grant
options for groups, roles, and `PUBLIC`, and options outside `privileges`.

**Drift.** Refresh compares the defaults of the exact owner, schema, object type, and grantee with the state, and the
next plan restores the configuration with the statements above: a default granted outside Terraform is revoked, and a
revoked one is granted again. Schema defaults and database-wide defaults are separate tuples, and the privileges of
existing objects are never read. A missing database, owner, schema, or grantee removes the grant from state, and the
next plan creates it again.

The following example shows the statements an update runs, each with the database it runs in: the existence
checks and the catalog read, the changes, and the read that verifies them.

### Replacing a Default Privilege

The role `readers` receives `INSERT` instead of `SELECT` on tables that `loader` creates in `serving` from now on:

```terraform
resource "redshift_default_privileges" "loader_tables" {
  database_name = "warehouse"
  owner         = "loader"
  schema_name   = "serving"
  object_type   = "TABLES"
  grantee_type  = "ROLE"
  grantee       = "readers"
  privileges    = ["INSERT"] # was ["SELECT"]
}
```

